matical background. Unavailable originals remain disclosed UNVERIFIED source assumptions. |\\n19: | `structure` | package | original_context_note | Verify the actual AND dependency graph, unique producers, absence of cycles and faithful exported statements. Retain every original H and disclose unused H. Internal results are proved, never new roots. |\\n20: | `statement_closure` | closure | output_notes | Inputs and output state the mathematical content, objects, domains, quantifiers and conditions their consumers need. Check actual object occurrences and definitions. No missing identification can be supplied by an absent producer proof or a paper equation number. |\\n21: | `dependency_closure` | closure | dependency_and_local_assumption_check | Check direct input use, earlier local facts, witness existence and proper discharge of assumptions. No circular dependency, future fact or escaped local object is allowed. Mathematically checked routine background may supplement the packet without prior native lookup. |\\n22: | `argument` | inference | dependency_and_local_assumption_check | Verify the complete implication and every output clause at ordinary rigorous mathematical granularity, including material limiting operations, domains and regularity. Compress correct routine reasoning; locate a real gap rather than requiring a transcript of elementary steps. |\\n23: | `applicability` | inference | source_application_check | Check actual instantiations and all material premises of invoked E/M or routine background facts, with grounds in inputs or earlier local derivations. Required bridges are proved. A familiar theorem name or a proposed Lean tactic supplies no mathematical justification. |\\n24: \\n25: ### Scope and mathematical boundary\\n26: \\n27: Global review owns target/H fidelity, definitions, source statements and graph\\n28: consistency. Each local reviewer owns the conditional implication at its R\\n29: node. Conditional packet replay has a different input boundary: these statements are conditional premises.\\n30: The local reviewer does not re-prove an input N or re-audit its source paper;\\n31: the graph and that producer's review establish those separate obligations.\\n32: Conversely, an input N exports only its full statement, not unexported facts\\n33: from the producer's body. The fifth local criterion, packet_replay, assesses\\n34: sufficiency under this exact boundary. The controller records execution\\n35: identity and exposure independently of the mathematical judgment.\\n36: \\n37: Review correctness rather than the number of displayed lines. Ordinary valid\\n38: textbook compression is allowed. Material missing hypotheses, witnesses,\\n39: constructions, scope discharges or core arguments are not implementation gaps.\\n40: Return them for located author repair and fresh review. No theorem needs to be re-proved\\n41: when its legitimate mathematical source and actual application suffice.\\n42: \\n43: The domain-neutral elementary-language-4 profile retains equality, supplied\\n44: definitions, finite arithmetic/order reasoning and finite sums. It is syntax\\n45: and elementary calculation policy, not a subject vocabulary or theorem list.\\n46: Objects from any field use problem parameters, D definitions or scoped locals.\\n47: Neither an author-written calculation list nor a familiar symbol adds a theorem.\\n48: \\n49: ### Mathematical background: mathematical-background-v2-1\\n50: \\n51: A reviewer may positively assess a routine, local mathematical fact without\\n52: first finding its Mathlib declaration. Record its precise mathematical\\n53: statement, actual argument location, available condition grounds and a short\\n54: mathematical justification in a background note. Use kind mathematical for an\\n55: ordinary background result and elementary for a useful elementary completion.\\n56: This is an actual mathematical judgment, not a future TODO or a claim of\\n57: native verification. Do not record every algebraic operation.\\n58: \\n59: Eligibility requires a correct standard local step with all premises already\\n60: available, no new H and no substantial new core proof or changed route.\\n61: Important external tools remain explicit E inputs; a missing project result\\n62: or uncertain source cannot be waived as background. If an explicitly supplied\\n63: native mathlib note is retained, its full bound evidence is still validated.\\n64: No field-specific whitelist determines eligibility.\\n65: \\n66: The accepted authority is immutable author NL0 plus its hash-bound mathematical\\n67: review supplement. It is a natural-language audit under the mathematical-background policy, not a\\n68: detailed-NL0 format-3 native-background receipt. At nl02lean, workers must discharge all necessary\\n69: facts by actual Lean proofs or checked library use; a note never becomes an\\n70: unproved H, axiom or extra external assumption. The exact implementation may\\n71: differ while preserving the mathematical meaning.\\n72: \\n73: ### Actual review and result qualification\\n74: \\n75: Independent reviewers assess the actual content; schema validation does not\\n76: prove mathematics. An unexamined item remains unread/read. A mathematical\\n77: uncertainty is assessed UNVERIFIED, not PASS. Missing or conflicting assessments\\n78: block acceptance. Ordinary source-access deferral follows the conditional-source\\n79: policy and must appear in final warnings; it cannot yield unqualified PASS.\\n80: \\n81: Use short located reasons for straightforward PASS items and enough detail to\\n82: make a defect or uncertainty actionable. Avoid duplicate explanations across\\n83: criteria. A concise report still covers every required mathematical check.\\n84: Do not read author self-check notes, old verdicts, other R proof bodies, other\\n85: case results or personal proof skills to manufacture independent approval.\\n86: <!-- yui-nl0-acceptance:end -->\\n87: \\n88: ## Execution protocol\\n89: \\n90: 1. Freeze the original problem, spec and NL0. A separate fresh global reviewer\\n91:    reads the exhaustive global-overview.json, relevant canonical content and\\n92:    source evidence. Review each D and selected E/M; the overview is data, not\\n93:    an assessment. Do not infer absence from a partial page.\\n94: 2. Complete global source/graph review before dispatching local R reviews.\\n95:    Independent R reviewers use the current R-node count as their default slot\\n96:    limit, or the user's explicit positive N cap.\\n97:    Each receives only its own packet and mathematical-NL0 rules. Review full arguments,\\n98:    actual applications and definitions; input N statements are conditional.\\n99: 3. Prefer one complete artifact with an explicit coverage map. Use\\n100:    record_review_item and read_review_progress only for useful checkpoints.\\n101:    record_background_note is optional; a complete artifact may include its\\n102:    actual notes directly. Batch reading several declared fragments is available.\\n103: 4. Each local report has inference, packet_sha256, verdict, notes, checks,\\n104:    findings and optional background_notes. The checks map contains exactly\\n105:    statement_closure, dependency_closure, argument, applicability, packet_replay.\\n106:    Each row has verdict, notes and resolving packet locations. E applications\\n107:    additionally retain actual binding, per-hypothesis, conclusion and bridge\\n108:    assessments; this is mathematical applicability, not a Lean API inventory.\\n109: 5. Global reports have target_review, definition_reviews, source_reviews,\\n110:    structure_review and findings. Each E adds citation_checks for\\n111:    literature_identity, result_location, statement_fidelity,\\n112:    context_completeness, version_and_eligibility. Inaccessible primary text\\n113:    leaves blocked checks UNVERIFIED while remaining assessment continues.\\n114: 6. A blocking finding needs its actual location, explanation and a short exact\\n115:    quotation from that location. Preserve the defect and untouched coverage.\\n116:    Return located mathematical repairs to the author; review changed scopes\\n117:    independently. Do not resample a mathematical rejection or silently erase it.\\n118: 7. The collector checks complete coverage, consistency, exact bytes and actual\\n119:    independent execution before producing the audit-format-4 receipt.\\n120:    Old detailed-NL0 format-3 receipts are not silently upgraded. Inherited format-4 judgments retain\\n121:    their original source/rule hashes, exposure and costs.\\n122: \\n123: ## Sources, scopes and final handoff\\n124: \\n125: Use [external theorem review](external-theorems.md) and\\n126: [conditional sources](conditional-sources.md) for source identity, exact\\n127: statements and warnings. Reuse adequate original text; several theorem uses\\n128: may share one staged readback. Each application still discharges its own\\n129: premises. No successful download or guessed Mathlib availability is approval.\\n130: \\n131: Use [logical rules](logical-rules.md) for local scopes and\\n132: [background notes](background-notes.md) for the mathematical supplement.\\n133: The ordinary complete result remains relative to explicit E assumptions.\\n134: An inaccessible E original permits only a source-qualified result. Translation\\n135: retains this exact audit and supplement until reviewed mathematical changes\\n136: invalidate affected scopes. Typed interfaces, native checking, standalone\\n137: assembly and independent semantic Audit B are separate later gates.\\n138: \\n139: ## Paywalled or inaccessible primary text\\n140: \\n141: Follow [conditional sources](conditional-sources.md). Preserve the complete\\n142: secondary statement and actual access evidence; unavailable source checks\\n143: stay UNVERIFIED. Continue other work only with an explicitly qualified source\\n144: assumption, and report warn.json/warn.md. No unqualified full PASS is granted.\"}"}
{"status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"path\": \"nl0/templates/authoring.md\", \"total_lines\": 179, \"start\": 1, \"end\": 179, \"start_column\": 0, \"end_column\": 66, \"requested_end\": 250, \"has_more\": false, \"next_start\": null, \"next_column\": 0, \"next_end\": null, \"text\": \"1: # NL0 construction and tool template\\n2: \\n3: Use [Audit A's shared criteria](../references/audit-a.md#shared-mathematical-acceptance-requirements)\\n4: as the mathematical authority. This template explains data/tool construction;\\n5: it is not a parallel acceptance policy. Read the [schema](../references/nl0-format.md),\\n6: [construction guide](../references/nl0-authoring.md), and the complete neutral\\n7: [submission](submission-example.md), [source application](source-application-example.md)\\n8: and [quantified implication](quantified-implication-example.md) examples.\\n9: These examples show format and derivations, not certified benchmark answers.\\n10: \\n11: The complete [guarded-definition example](guarded-definition-example.md) shows\\n12: how to organize a target that includes an object's existence and a property\\n13: of that object. For a real a let Q(r) mean r=a. R001 first proves N001, unique\\n14: existence of a Q witness, without using w. D002 then defines w as that unique\\n15: witness with requires=[N001]. R002 takes N001 as a direct input and proves the\\n16: full target: unique existence and w=a. Listing D002 in the global spec does\\n17: not let R001 use it before its guard. Do not hide an unproved guard in a\\n18: restricted domain when the frozen target requires a statement on the full\\n19: original domain. This pattern adds no new assumption or mathematical source.\\n20: \\n21: ## Construct one coherent candidate\\n22: \\n23: 1. For `target` and `definitions`, extract the original H, ambient parameters\\n24:    and global typed definitions. Preserve the original source locations. Do\\n25:    not change a parameter's mathematical kind while fixing its metadata.\\n26: 2. For `statement_closure`, design N/R interfaces from what their\\n27:    consumers actually need. Put the complete formulas, quantified conditions\\n28:    and space identifications into the exported statement, not only its body.\\n29: 3. For `dependency_closure` and `argument`, construct\\n30:    each proof unit alongside its premises. List exact source inputs or display\\n31:    a prior local derivation for every substantive fact beyond the fixed\\n32:    calculations. Split when local context changes; avoid a giant S whose\\n33:    changing objects must later be repaired one at a time.\\n34: 4. For `sources` and `applicability`, inspect each selected native/primary\\n35:    source, preserve its complete contract and ground each instantiated premise.\\n36:    A signature lookup or a sentence claiming a hypothesis is true is not proof\\n37:    of that hypothesis. Reuse an adequate proved source rather than re-proving\\n38:    it; a project-specific bridge still requires its displayed derivation.\\n39: 5. For object declarations and local scopes under dependency_closure, introduce objects and conditional premises\\n40:    at their actual first use. Keep witness bases and local F dependencies\\n41:    correct. Preserve exact coefficients and ranges when copying calculations.\\n42: 6. Run check_draft, correct its actual located records, inspect every own\\n43:    conditional packet, and report readiness below. The independent auditor\\n44:    applies the same criteria after definitive submission.\\n45: \\n46: ## Canonical data shapes\\n47: \\n48: Submit {spec,graph} using the schema, or preserve an own draft with\\n49: {base_candidate:N,patches:[...]}. The controller binds identity, input/pin\\n50: hashes, fixed language and exact native metadata; it does not rewrite proof\\n51: content. Use read_draft with a narrow pointer after an edit rejection.\\n52: Unique existing @ID/@symbol selectors work for own-stage reads/patches;\\n53: inserting array elements still uses numeric indices or /-. Audit evidence\\n54: uses standard JSON pointers instead of this selector extension.\\n55: \\n56: For multiline changes prefer read_draft(candidate=N,pointer=\\\"/graph/inferences/@R001/argument/@S001/text\\\",\\n57: start_line=1,end_line=20). It returns the actual numbered lines, total_lines\\n58: and complete text_sha256. Use an edit with exactly\\n59: {op:\\\"replace_lines\\\",path:\\\"/graph/inferences/@R001/argument/@S001/text\\\",start_line:4,end_line:5,\\n60: text:\\\"replacement including its intended line endings\\\",text_sha256:\\\"the returned hash\\\"}.\\n61: Ranges are positive and inclusive. Unselected text and line endings are\\n62: preserved; stale hashes are rejected. Include a line terminator when a\\n63: nonempty replacement precedes retained lines. Empty text deletes the range.\\n64: All ordinary validation and later Audit A still apply.\\n65: For reads only, an end beyond the string returns its remaining existing lines\\n66: and the actual range; the start must exist and at most 250 li…2525 tokens truncated…: gives \\\\(\\\\dim_H F\\\\leq D+1\\\\).\\n93: \\n94: ### Lower bound when \\\\(D>0\\\\)\\n95: \\n96: Fix \\\\(0<s<D\\\\). Then \\\\(H^s(E)=\\\\infty\\\\), so\\n97: \\\\(H^s_\\\\delta(E)\\\\) becomes arbitrarily large as \\\\(\\\\delta\\\\downarrow0\\\\).\\n98: We claim that, for every \\\\(\\\\delta>0\\\\),\\n99: \\n100: \\\\[\\n101: H^s_\\\\delta(E)\\\\leq H^{s+1}_\\\\delta(F).                 \\\\tag{1}\\n102: \\\\]\\n103: \\n104: Take an arbitrary countable \\\\(\\\\delta\\\\)-cover \\\\((V_i)_{i\\\\geq0}\\\\) of\\n105: \\\\(F\\\\), discarding empty members. Define\\n106: \\\\(\\\\rho_i=\\\\operatorname{diam}V_i\\\\),\\n107: \\\\(A_i=\\\\pi_{\\\\mathbb R^n}(V_i)\\\\), and\\n108: \\\\(B_i=\\\\pi_{\\\\mathbb R}(V_i)\\\\). Coordinate projections are 1-Lipschitz,\\n109: so both projected diameters are at most \\\\(\\\\rho_i\\\\leq\\\\delta\\\\).\\n110: For \\\\(\\\\varepsilon>0\\\\), put\\n111: \\n112: \\\\[\\n113: \\\\eta_i=\\\\frac{\\\\varepsilon2^{-i-1}}{1+\\\\rho_i^s}>0.\\n114: \\\\]\\n115: \\n116: Enclose \\\\(B_i\\\\) in an open interval \\\\(J_i\\\\) of length at most\\n117: \\\\(\\\\rho_i+\\\\eta_i\\\\): its supremum minus infimum is at most \\\\(\\\\rho_i\\\\),\\n118: and an enlargement of total width \\\\(\\\\eta_i\\\\) suffices. This avoids\\n119: assuming that the arbitrary covering sets or their projections are measurable.\\n120: \\n121: For each \\\\(t\\\\in[0,1]\\\\), the sets \\\\(A_i\\\\) for which \\\\(t\\\\in J_i\\\\)\\n122: cover \\\\(E\\\\). Indeed, a point \\\\((x,t)\\\\in F\\\\) belongs to some \\\\(V_i\\\\),\\n123: giving \\\\(x\\\\in A_i\\\\) and \\\\(t\\\\in B_i\\\\subset J_i\\\\). Their diameters\\n124: are at most \\\\(\\\\rho_i\\\\). Consequently, with \\\\(c=H^s_\\\\delta(E)\\\\),\\n125: \\n126: \\\\[\\n127: c\\\\leq\\\\sum_i\\\\rho_i^s\\\\mathbf1_{J_i}(t)\\\\quad(t\\\\in[0,1]).\\n128: \\\\]\\n129: \\n130: The constant \\\\(c\\\\) is finite: compactness gives a finite cover of \\\\(E\\\\)\\n131: by balls of diameter at most \\\\(\\\\delta\\\\), with finite \\\\(s\\\\)-cost.\\n132: Each summand is nonnegative and measurable since \\\\(J_i\\\\) is open.\\n133: Integrate over \\\\([0,1]\\\\), of Lebesgue measure one, and use Tonelli's\\n134: theorem for this nonnegative series. Since\\n135: \\\\(|J_i\\\\cap[0,1]|\\\\leq\\\\rho_i+\\\\eta_i\\\\),\\n136: \\n137: \\\\[\\n138: \\\\begin{aligned}\\n139: c&\\\\leq\\\\sum_i\\\\rho_i^s|J_i\\\\cap[0,1]|\\\\\\\\\\n140:  &\\\\leq\\\\sum_i\\\\rho_i^{s+1}+\\\\sum_i\\\\eta_i\\\\rho_i^s\\n141:  \\\\leq\\\\sum_i\\\\rho_i^{s+1}+\\\\varepsilon.\\n142: \\\\end{aligned}\\n143: \\\\]\\n144: \\n145: If the first series is infinite the desired inequality is immediate;\\n146: otherwise let \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\downarrow0\\\\). Take the infimum over the\\n147: arbitrary covers to obtain (1). As its left side is unbounded at small\\n148: scales, \\\\(H^{s+1}(F)=\\\\infty\\\\), giving \\\\(\\\\dim_H F\\\\geq s+1\\\\).\\n149: Letting \\\\(s\\\\uparrow D\\\\) proves \\\\(\\\\dim_H F\\\\geq D+1\\\\).\\n150: \\n151: ### Lower bound when \\\\(D=0\\\\)\\n152: \\n153: Choose \\\\(x_0\\\\in E\\\\). The map \\\\(t\\\\mapsto(x_0,t)\\\\) is an isometric\\n154: embedding of \\\\([0,1]\\\\) into \\\\(F\\\\), so dimension monotonicity gives\\n155: \\\\(\\\\dim_H F\\\\geq\\\\dim_H[0,1]\\\\). This interval has dimension one:\\n156: equal-length covers gave the upper bound above. For \\\\(0<s<1\\\\), any\\n157: \\\\(\\\\delta\\\\)-cover with diameters \\\\(\\\\rho_i\\\\leq\\\\delta\\\\) satisfies\\n158: \\\\(\\\\sum_i\\\\rho_i\\\\geq1\\\\). To see this, enclose its nonempty members\\n159: in open intervals of lengths at most \\\\(\\\\rho_i+\\\\varepsilon2^{-i-1}\\\\),\\n160: use countable subadditivity of length to get\\n161: \\\\(1\\\\leq\\\\sum_i\\\\rho_i+\\\\varepsilon\\\\), and let \\\\(\\\\varepsilon\\\\downarrow0\\\\).\\n162: Hence \\\\(\\\\sum_i\\\\rho_i^s\\\\geq\\\\delta^{s-1}\\\\sum_i\\\\rho_i\\n163: \\\\geq\\\\delta^{s-1}\\\\to\\\\infty\\\\) as \\\\(\\\\delta\\\\downarrow0\\\\).\\n164: This proves the lower bound one. Thus \\\\(\\\\dim_H F\\\\geq1=D+1\\\\),\\n165: including when \\\\(n=0\\\\).\\n166: \\n167: Together the bounds establish the required equality.\"}"}
{"status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"path\": \"nl0/references/nl0-authoring.md\", \"total_lines\": 220, \"start\": 1, \"end\": 220, \"start_column\": 0, \"end_column\": 78, \"requested_end\": 220, \"has_more\": false, \"next_start\": null, \"next_column\": 0, \"next_end\": null, \"text\": \"1: # Construct NL0 against Audit A\\n2: \\n3: [Audit A's shared requirements](audit-a.md#shared-mathematical-acceptance-requirements)\\n4: are the sole mathematical acceptance authority. This document is an execution\\n5: guide. Follow the table IDs, not an independently maintained approximation of\\n6: what the auditor might want. The clean carrier supplies that exact frozen\\n7: section to both roles. [The schema](nl0-format.md) describes serialization;\\n8: [the author template](../templates/authoring.md) gives tool shapes and examples.\\n9: The elementary permissions come from the same controller-owned foundation.\\n10: Use ordinary rigorous mathematical granularity. Explicitly register important\\n11: tools; eligible tiny background uses can be checked and recorded by Audit A\\n12: under [mathematical-background admission](background-notes.md). Do not pre-emptively\\n13: rebuild elementary theories or treat an omitted basic M ID as a substantive gap.\\n14: \\n15: ## 1. Understand and freeze the supplied work\\n16: \\n17: Read the original target and entire supplied proof. Identify its phases and\\n18: potential gaps, without adopting an alternative research route. Extract the\\n19: original H, parameters and definition pairs into spec.json. Preserve their\\n20: source locations. This supplies the `target` check; naming a condition inside\\n21: a parameter type does not satisfy its H inventory. Inspect material endpoint,\\n22: zero-object and ambient-dimension cases before building the graph.\\n23: Each H must itself state the complete given condition; phrases such as\\n24: \\\"as specified in the target\\\" do not supply that condition to a packet reader.\\n25: When an original GIVEN passage is already adequate, select its exact frozen\\n26: lines for `spec.hypotheses[i].statement`, using the same literal binding as D\\n27: definitions and S text. Preserve its origin and distinguish original givens\\n28: from desired conclusions and claims established later in the proof. Copying\\n29: does not turn those latter claims into hypotheses or certify fidelity.\\n30: \\n31: Inventory each object's construction guards before exporting it with a type.\\n32: If a guard is a result of this work, establish it in an earlier N producer\\n33: before using the guarded D; do not promise that a later argument will make the\\n34: current declaration well-defined. Alternatively specify an actually total\\n35: construction and prove its intended property separately. Do not add a missing\\n36: guard as an original H. This is graph organization along the given proof,\\n37: not permission to change that proof or its hypotheses.\\n38: When the original already gives a complete adequate definition, use the\\n39: explicit frozen-input line binding for D.definition if helpful. This preserves\\n40: the selected text and its provenance without granting a property. Rewriting\\n41: notation still requires a complete faithful definition and its declarations.\\n42: Use the [complete neutral construction pattern](../templates/guarded-definition-example.md)\\n43: when P includes both a well-definedness claim and a property of the object:\\n44: prove the guard without the object, then use that guard as a direct input to\\n45: the object-using inference. A global list's textual order is not graph order.\\n46: \\n47: ## 2. Design outputs from their consumers\\n48: \\n49: Follow the existing proof phases to choose N/R units. Work backward from P:\\n50: what exact formulas, maps, parameter ranges and representation identifications\\n51: will each consumer actually use? Put those in its input proposition's exported\\n52: statement. Read that statement without the producer body. If the consumer\\n53: cannot reconstruct a needed fact from it, enlarge the producer's proved\\n54: output or give the consumer another genuine direct input. Then write the\\n55: producer under precisely its inputs. This implements `statement_closure`,\\n56: the grouped argument and applicability criteria; it is not permission to invent a stronger theorem.\\n57: \\n58: Start another S when the local parameters or purpose change; export a reused\\n59: result as N and a within-R result as a complete F. Avoid one giant copied S\\n60: followed by a long sequence of isolated symbol patches. Copy exact supplied\\n61: calculations through task-line text binding when helpful, then close the\\n62: copied unit's actual declarations and premises. Keep all required coefficients,\\n63: normalizations, limiting arguments and exceptional cases.\\n64: Retain every calculation needed for P and the supplied core route. An\\n65: independent optional comment/corollary outside that dependency may be omitted;\\n66: note a material omission in the existing context note. This never licenses\\n67: dropping a needed derivation or weakening an output. Any retained claim must\\n68: itself have its complete grounds.\\n69: \\n70: ## 3. Construct the proof and its factual grounds together\\n71: \\n72: An isomorphism or structural identification supplies its stated maps and\\n73: relations, not every subsequent dimension/rank claim by name alone. For a\\n74: needed count, display the basis, spanning/independence and finite count, or\\n75: invoke an appropriate verified source. Likewise regularity closure and\\n76: differentiation rules need their actual grounds; a familiar operation name\\n77: does not extend the fixed calculation profile. Prefer an adequate verified\\n78: background input to unnecessarily rebuilding its whole theory.\\n79: \\n80: For each S, identify its substantive direct inputs and local grounds while\\n81: writing. Ordinary correct elementary reasoning may be compressed; routine mathematical facts may receive concise mathematically reviewed\\n82: background notes, without exact Mathlib lookup. Check the\\n83: unit at that point; do not postpone its source inventory until after the whole\\n84: proof. Read `dependency_closure` and `argument` as construction obligations, not final assurances to fill in later.\\n85: \\n86: If a fact needs an M/E, inspect the real pinned/primary source, register its\\n87: full contract, and write its actual application. Follow `sources` and\\n88: `applicability`: substitute the actual function/domain/scalars and ground each\\n89: premise in an available input or displayed derivation. A source-hypothesis\\n90: field saying a condition holds is not itself a derivation. Prefer an already\\n91: proved adequate general theorem to rebuilding it. A specialized bridge still\\n92: needs its local proof. For E follow [external-source review](external-theorems.md).\\n93: For inaccessible text follow Audit A's [retrieval/deferred checks](audit-a.md#paywalled-or-inaccessible-primary-text).\\n94: \\n95: M is optional. Do not search native declarations for every routine fact before\\n96: NL0 review; use precise mathematical reasoning and the background-note policy.\\n97: If M is explicitly selected, use the actual pinned declaration as the full source contract, including\\n98: its binders, implicit arguments and typeclass premises. Do not insert an\\n99: extra condition into that contract or omit a native premise. Keep a specialized\\n100: application distinct from the native statement and justify its substitutions.\\n101: The probe supplies canonical source metadata; it does not discharge the\\n102: application's premises or certify the local proof.\\n103: \\n104: Write the exact conclusion obtained at an application before using it at a\\n105: different domain, measure, carrier or codomain. Any restriction, extension or\\n106: identification requires its own available ground. If instead deriving a fact\\n107: by a named property's definition, supply that definition and display the\\n108: instantiated clause and ensuing reasoning. Do not register a new source just\\n109: because a fully displayed definition-based derivation also has a standard\\n110: name. Prove each added consequence under all its cases; omit an unnecessary\\n111: extra claim rather than leave its proof incomplete.\\n112: \\n113: Introduce an auxiliary object at its actual first use, with its kind,\\n114: existence basis and scope. Keep object dependencies in `objects` and earlier\\n115: proof IDs in `from_local`. Preserve the object's type and all uses when\\n116: renaming it. Apply `definitions` and the grouped `statement_closure` check to the actual prose.\\n117: Use [logical packaging](logical-rules.md) for quantified and conditional work.\\n118: \\n119: ## 4. Inspect the actual stored candidate\\n120: \\n121: Use check_draft for structural feedback and read_draft for actual changed\\n122: records. Continue from the same-stage candidate after a rejected edit. Stable\\n123: unique @ID/@symbol selectors are available in own-stage reads/edits, not in\\n124: Audit A evidence pointers. Format work requires a concrete edit or source\\n125: query rather than repeated progress reports.\\n126: For multiline text use read_draft's numbered line view and its complete-string\\n127: hash with replace_lines; avoid retyping an escaped old paragraph merely to\\n128: make a small edit. Re-read after a stale hash and use existing @IDs after\\n129: array changes. No editor infers missing mathematics.\\n130: \\n131: Read each own conditional packet after structural checking. It supplies only\\n132: that R's actual premises, definitions, fixed logic, output and own argument.\\n133: Use the shared criteria to inspect these contents, including each output's\\n134: consumer-facing statement, rather than remembering what the full manuscript\\n135: or another producer established. This is the same author's inspection, not an\\n136: extra independent reviewer and not a second Audit A.\\n137: Apply Audit A's shared scope rule: an actual direct N is assumed in this\\n138: conditional implication; do not re-prove its producer or read an ancestor\\n139: body here. The global graph must still contain that producer, and a needed\\n140: fact absent from the exported statement must be established explicitly.\\n141: In the existing original_context_note explicitly check the actual D records'\\n142: types and guards against their available producers, as well as H/target and\\n143: source fidelity. In the inference notes compare each source's exact delivered\\n144: conclusion with the next line that consumes it. These are inspections of the\\n145: stored proof, not another copy of its argument.\\n146: \\n147: Record concise located context/output/inference notes using the existing\\n148: fields. Their criterion grouping comes directly from Audit A. Report readiness\\n149: as no_known_issue, unresolved or not_checked and preserve located open issues.\\n150: A complete set of notes with an unresolved issue cannot release the proof.\\n151: Resolve it in the actual stored argument, or explicitly justify withdrawing\\n152: an erroneous self-finding; reference the resolution location. Do not erase an\\n153: old issue by omitting it. Python validates these states and bindings, not the\\n154: truth of the author's mathematical judgment. A claim of no_known_issue can\\n155: still fail the subsequent independent Audit A.\\n156: \\n157: ### Routine completion and substantive proof gaps\\n158: \\n159: A needed statement in the supplied proof is initially a proof obligation:\\n160: derive it locally, export a proved N, or supply an exactly verified source.\\n161: The fact that the current draft has not done so yet is not a mathematical\\n162: obstruction. Missing inventory, source registration or exposition is work to\\n163: complete, not by itself a terminal gap report. If reporting an actual obstacle,\\n164: identify the concrete implication and the mathematical difficulty encountered\\n165: during normal understanding, checking and completion, rather than only noting\\n166: that no proof/source has yet been entered. This imposes no fixed time/attempt\\n167: count and requires neither a counterexample nor a proof of irreparability.\\n168: \\n169: When writing NL0, expand the argument along the proof strategy already present\\n170: in the original work. Actively supply routine omitted derivations, correct\\n171: verifiable typographical errors and non-substantive local mistakes, and write\\n172: the complete argument into NL0 without interrupting the user for each item.\\n173: Completion must preserve the original target, the original theorem hypotheses\\n174: and the core proof strategy.\\n175: \\n176: If a step remains blocked after normal understanding, checking and completion\\n177: attempts, carefully assess the obstacle: is it work needed for ordinary\\n178: rigorous expansion, or a substantive gap in the original proof? The model\\n179: should make this judgment from the mathematical content and actual progress;\\n180: no fixed elapsed-time limit or attempt count is a mathematical criterion.\\n181: \\n182: If the obstacle is judged to be a substantive proof gap, stop NL0 construction\\n183: for the current proof, preserve the completed work, and report the gap's\\n184: location, the unsupported implication and the grounds for that judgment.\\n185: Do not automatically start new research on the original problem, seek a new\\n186: core proof strategy, or modify the original theorem to make the proof pass.\\n187: \\n188: Recognizing a gap does not require finding a counterexample or proving that\\n189: the gap is irreparable. If the only established fact is that completion has\\n190: not been achieved, and its substantive nature cannot be judged reliably,\\n191: report an \\\"unresolved suspected proof gap\\\" rather than declaring the original\\n192: theorem false. Prolonged runtime, network or tool faults, and model capability\\n193: limitations alone are not evidence of a mathematical error.\\n194: \\n195: In the isolated NL0 carrier, unfinished source registration, JSON bookkeeping or\\n196: ordinary drafting calls for the next actual query, draft or local edit, not an\\n197: obstacle report. Keep the own draft and continue within its remaining budget. A\\n198: terminal mathematical-gap report must identify a genuinely unsupported\\n199: mathematical implication and its grounds, rather than merely restating that\\n200: the requested artifact is not finished. A stage outcome is not whole-task\\n201: completion; ordinary failed candidates return to bounded correction/review.\\n202: \\n203: Incorporate completed corrections into the argument and its actual dependency\\n204: records. Audit A reviews that revised version; existing hash binding and\\n205: invalidation rules still apply.\\n206: \\n207: \\n208: ## 5. Freeze and hand over\\n209: \\n210: When there is no known unfinished obligation, submit the exact candidate bound\\n211: to the author notes and shared acceptance version. If the budget ends first,\\n212: preserve the draft as unfinished; do not call a known-incomplete artifact a\\n213: complete proof. A diagnostic audit of an unfinished draft, if requested,\\n214: remains separate from a qualifying first-acceptance audit.\\n215: \\n216: Freeze source/graph hashes and generate views and an UNVERIFIED scaffold.\\n217: Audit A receives the original problem for fidelity and fresh per-R packets for\\n218: local replay; it receives no author notes or prior review feedback. Its actual\\n219: judgments determine acceptance under the same shared criteria. Structural\\n220: validation, author readiness and mathematical acceptance are distinct results.\"}"}

